Yes. A useful way to think about it is that a transformation changes the representation, scale, topology, semantics, or resolution of data while ideally preserving some invariant you care about.

Transformation family	What it changes	Examples
Scaling	Magnitude/range	min–max scaling, z-score, logarithm, power transform, percentage, per-capita normalization
Translation / offset	Baseline	subtract mean, inflation-adjust dollars, center coordinates
Rotation / projection	Orientation or viewpoint	PCA rotation, coordinate transforms, camera projection
Reshaping	Arrangement without necessarily changing values	wide↔long, matrix↔tensor, flatten, pivot, transpose
Dimensionality	Number of descriptive axes	PCA, embeddings, feature selection, compression
Aggregation	Many observations → fewer summaries	mean, median, sum, histogram, rolling average
Disaggregation	Coarse → finer representation	annual→monthly estimates, national→regional decomposition
Granularity mapping	Resolution	seconds→minutes, pixels→regions, species→genus, ZIP→county
Categorization	Continuous/complex → discrete class	age→age band, probability→risk class, text→topic
Binning / quantization	Precision	temperatures into ranges, RGB to fewer color levels
Encoding	Representation system	one-hot, ordinal encoding, UTF-8, binary, tokenization
Decoding	Representation → interpreted state	token→word, bytes→image, code→symbol
Ranking	Absolute values → relative order	percentile, leaderboard, decile, dominance ordering
Thresholding	Continuous → decision	probability > 0.7 → positive, edge/no-edge
Filtering	Removes selected components	low-pass, spam filter, outlier removal, SQL WHERE
Smoothing	Reduces local variation	moving average, Gaussian smoothing, LOESS
Differencing	State → change	x_t-x_{t-1}, velocity from position, returns from prices
Integration / accumulation	Changes → state	cumulative sum, distance from velocity, lifetime revenue
Derivative-like transformation	Rate of change	slope, acceleration, gradient
Temporal alignment	Time relationship	lag, lead, resampling, synchronization, interpolation
Windowing	Local context	rolling 7-day average, sliding image patch, sequence chunk
Frequency transform	Time/space → frequencies	Fourier transform, wavelets, spectrograms
Spatial transform	Geometry	crop, translate, rotate, warp, georeference
Coordinate transform	Reference frame	Cartesian↔polar, latitude/longitude↔map projection
Structural mapping	Organization	list→tree, table→graph, graph→matrix, hierarchy→flat taxonomy
Relational transformation	Connections rather than nodes	adjacency matrix, co-occurrence network, correlation graph
Graph transformation	Network structure	contraction, community detection, line graph, graph embedding
Topological transformation	Connectivity/shape properties	connected components, holes, persistence, neighborhood structure
Join / fusion	Multiple sources → one structure	SQL join, sensor fusion, multimodal alignment
Split / factorization	One structure → components	train/test split, matrix factorization, source separation
Decomposition	Composite signal → constituent parts	trend + seasonality + noise, eigenvectors, ICA
Feature extraction	Raw signal → informative descriptors	edges, frequency peaks, sentiment score, texture
Feature construction	Existing features → new ones	BMI from height/weight, ratios, interactions, polynomial terms
Interaction transformation	Variables → relationships	xy, covariance, correlations, pairwise distances
Distance transformation	Objects → similarity/dissimilarity	Euclidean distance, cosine similarity, edit distance
Kernel transformation	Data → implicit richer geometry	RBF kernel, polynomial kernel
Embedding	Complex object → vector	words, images, users, graphs represented in latent space
Latent-state inference	Observations → hidden causes	factor models, HMMs, Bayesian latent variables
Probability transformation	Scores/beliefs	odds, log-odds, likelihood, posterior probability
Calibration	Raw score → reliable probability	isotonic regression, Platt scaling
Standardization	Different measurement systems → comparable form	z-scores, unit normalization
Unit transformation	Measurement vocabulary	°F↔°C, meters↔feet, nominal dollars↔real dollars
Semantic transformation	Meaning representation	sentence→intent, event→cause category, behavior→strategy
Abstraction	Specific → general	Labrador→dog→mammal→organism
Instantiation	General → specific	“vehicle”→truck→specific truck
Ontology mapping	One conceptual system → another	ICD code↔clinical concept, job title↔skill taxonomy
Label transformation	Classification vocabulary	merge classes, split classes, relabel, hierarchical labels
Logical transformation	Statement form	AND/OR/NOT, implication, Boolean simplification
Symbolic transformation	Expression structure	algebraic simplification, substitution, rewriting
Permutation	Order	shuffle, sort, reorder axes
Sampling	Population → subset	random sample, stratified sample, downsampling
Upsampling	Sparse → denser representation	interpolation, synthetic samples, image enlargement
Stochastic transformation	Adds controlled randomness	noise injection, Monte Carlo sampling, augmentation
Masking	Removes/obscures selected information	missing-value masks, attention masks, privacy redaction
Compression	Rich representation → smaller representation	JPEG, PCA, quantization, summaries
Expansion	Compact representation → richer space	polynomial basis, decoding, generated features
Canonicalization	Many equivalent forms → one standard form	lowercase text, standardized dates, canonical URLs
Deduplication	Repeated entities → single identity	record linkage, fuzzy matching
Imputation	Missing → estimated values	median fill, interpolation, model-based fill
Residualization	Remove explained component	actual − predicted, detrending, confound removal
Baseline correction	Relative-to-reference representation	anomaly from normal, change from control
Contrast transformation	Difference between conditions	treatment−control, before−after, signal/background
Ratio transformation	Relative magnitude	debt/income, signal/noise, conversion rate
Compositional transformation	Parts of a constrained whole	percentages, log-ratios, simplex transforms
Cumulative transformation	Point values → running state	CDF, cumulative sales, cumulative hazard
Order-statistic transformation	Distribution → ranks/quantiles	median, percentile, quantile normalization
Distribution transformation	Shape of statistical distribution	Box–Cox, log transform, Gaussianization
Model transformation	Data → parameters/function	regression coefficients, decision tree, neural network
Inverse transformation	Representation back toward source	exponentiating log values, inverse Fourier transform
Cross-modal transformation	One sensory/data form → another	speech→text, image→caption, sonar→spatial map
Domain transformation	Same phenomenon under another mathematical lens	time↔frequency, spatial↔spectral, node↔edge representation
Counterfactual transformation	Observed state → altered hypothetical state	“what if price were 10% lower?”
Simulation	Parameters/rules → synthetic observations	weather simulation, agent-based models
Generative transformation	Latent/code → new instance	embedding→image, prompt→text
Invariant transformation	Changes representation while deliberately preserving something	image rotation preserving object identity, permutation-invariant set encoding

The deeper distinction is what is preserved versus what is deliberately discarded.

For example:

\text{raw observations}
\rightarrow
\text{differences}
\rightarrow
\text{rates}
\rightarrow
\text{ratios}
\rightarrow
\text{normalized rates}
\rightarrow
\text{rank}
\rightarrow
\text{category}

The same dataset has now been viewed through six different mathematical “senses.”

And several particularly useful meta-types emerge.

Representation transforms change how something is written while trying to preserve its identity.

Resolution transforms change how finely you are looking.

Relational transforms stop studying the objects and start studying the spaces between the objects.

Derivative transforms stop asking what something is and ask how it is changing.

Latent transforms infer something invisible from visible manifestations.

Invariant transforms deliberately change many superficial properties while preserving the property you actually care about.

Lossy transforms intentionally throw away information to reveal structure.

That last one is important. Sometimes losing information increases usable information.

A photograph → edge map destroys color and texture, but suddenly geometry becomes conspicuous.

A stock-price series → percentage returns destroys absolute price level, but makes movement comparable.

A thousand behaviors → five behavioral archetypes destroys individuality, but exposes population structure.

So you can almost treat transformations as lenses:

D
\xrightarrow{T_1}
D'
\xrightarrow{T_2}
D''
\xrightarrow{T_3}
\cdots

And then your Delta Observatory idea becomes even stronger:

\boxed{\text{Compare what remains stable and what changes under multiple transformations}}

If a pattern survives scaling, normalization, different granularities, alternate categorizations, temporal windows, and different modalities, it is much harder to dismiss as an artifact of one representation.

That is essentially triangulation across transformation space.